So Mixed Straight not possible with current melds.\n\nAlternatively, \"Pure Straight\" (16 points) requires all three chows in same suit \u2014 not possible.\n\nSo maybe simpler patterns: \"All Chows\" (2) + \"No Honors\" (1) + \"Two Terminal Chows\" (1) (if I get another terminal chow) + \"Edge Wait\" (1) etc. might not reach 8. So I need to think about forming pungs or kongs for more points.\n\nLook at tile availability: W4 and W8 are pairs, so could aim for pungs. Unshown W4: 4 left, W8: 4 left. So chances to get third W4 or W8 are good. If I get pung of W4 or W8, that gives \"Pung of Terminals or Honors\" (1 point) but not terminal since W4 is not terminal. W8 is not terminal. So only 1 point. \"All Pungs\" (6 points) if I have four pungs \u2014 but I have two chows already, so not All Pungs.\n\nMaybe \"Seven Pairs\" (24 points) is possible? I have two pairs (W4, W8) and other singles. But to get Seven Pairs, I need seven distinct pairs. With 14 tiles, I have 8 hand tiles: currently two pairs and four singles. I would need to draw pairs for all singles, which is unlikely. Not feasible.\n\nGiven the hand is early (many tiles left), I should discard the least useful tile.\n\nEvaluate each discard:\n\n- Play W1: W1 is terminal, can form chow W1-W2-W3. I have W3, so need W2. Unshown W2: 4. Also W1 can be part of pair for pung? But only one. W1 is not useful for sequences with other tiles since only W1 and W3 are close. Discarding W1 loses chance for terminal chow and reduces flexibility.\n\n- Play W3: W3 can form chows with W1-W2 or W2-W4 or W4-W5. It's a flexible middle tile. Keeping it helps with sequences.\n\n- Play W4: I have a pair of W4. Discarding one breaks the pair, but I might still get another W4 later for pung? If I discard W4, I reduce pung chance but maybe improve sequences? With W3 and W4, I can form chow W2-W3-W4 or W3-W4-W5. So keeping one W4 is useful.\n\n- Play W8: Pair of W8. Similar to W4, but W8 is less flexible for chows since it's high number